00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:15.000
“Eat, Mawusi. Food will always comfort you when everything else is lost.”

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:28.000
I thought of my mother. I missed her. I don’t think I had ever missed her that badly before.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
I felt so lonely. It is strange to feel so alone surrounded by a crowd,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:45.000
But big cities have that strange quality: the larger they are, the smaller you feel. They make you feel like you don’t matter.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:54.000
“Eat, my sweet. Eat, and you’ll feel better.”

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
I looked around. In the absence of trees, I was not really spoiled for choice,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
But now was not the time to be picky, I was hungry, so anything would do.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.000
I saw a tiny white worm climbing on a statue and I gulped it.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:16.000
“Ugh!” I heard voices behind me. “Dear me, those migrants are disgusting.”

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.000
I turned around.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
“What do you mean?” I asked, my mouth still full,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.000
“And they have no manners.”

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
One plump grey pigeon was addressing two smaller ones, ignoring me completely.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:38.000
“Don’t you miss the good old days where we could feel at home here, having a peaceful luncheon undisturbed? This scene just spoiled my appetite.”

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
“Sorry”, I said, “I’m new here.”

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
“Obviously”, he sneered.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
“If you don’t eat worms, what do you eat, then?” I asked.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
“Bread, of course”, he replied haughtily. “We are civilised creatures. 

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:01.000
But I suppose there is no bread in your little jungle. No wonder you have to fall back on hunting smelly insects. How degrading.”

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.000
“Don’t be rude, Archie”, said one of the smaller pigeons.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:12.000
“She probably doesn’t understand us. Dooo yoouu haaave breaaad in aaa friii caaa?”

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:23.000
“I’m not stupid”, I said, “and of course I know bread. But that’s what humans eat. We just prefer fresh food. Fresh food.”

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
They all laughed, mimicking me.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
“I don’t know why you dislike them, Rosie”, added the first pigeon. “I find those migrants amusing.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.000
But let’s head back to the Ritz, shall we? They may have disposed of the afternoon tea’s scones by now.”

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
And they left me there, alone in the crowd, feeling stupid with the leftovers of my first English meal.
